{
  "case_id": "2010-09-329",
  "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:01 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I ask you about the deference that we should give to the briefs that have been filed in the First Circuit and the invitation brief in this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SOTOMAYOR",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So you think the same deference is owed to ANPRs as to the amicus briefs? What is your position on that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice ALITO",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 15,
        "start": 15
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "May I ask you a question about how the contract works in the situation in which a cardholder is found by -- was found by Chase to have defaulted by failing to make some payment other than payment on the Chase credit card?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Joseph R. PALMORE",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0000-0005",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "During the relevant time period, the Federal Reserve Board's Regulation Z did not require provision of a change-in-terms notice when a credit card issuer merely implemented a contractual penalty rate provision that had already been disclosed. This is clear from the staff commentary to the rule, from the Board's own statements in the Federal Register when discussing changes to this very rule, and finally from the amicus briefs filed by the Board in the First Circuit and in this Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KAGAN",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Palmore, what would the Board's position be on the following hypothetical: That a card issuer says when any of 50 different things happen, so 50 different triggering events, the -- the issuer can raise the rate anywhere up to 300 percent, so has complete discretion if any of a quite large number of triggering events occurs. And then one of those 50 triggering events occurs, and the card issuer says, okay, we'll raise the interest rate to 42 percent. Would there need to be notice for that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. PALMORE",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0001",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There's a specific staff comment, 6(a)(2)-11, which deals with the initial disclosure of penalty rate provisions, and it said there are two requirements of specificity. The specific maximum rate that may be applied must be disclosed, and the specific event or events that could lead to imposition of that specific maximum rate must be disclosed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. PALMORE",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Yes. Under staff comment 9(c)-1, the staff makes clear that if there's a general -- exercise of a change in rates pursuant to a general reservation of rights clause that's not specific with respect to the maximum rate that could apply or the specific triggering events that could lead to imposition of the maximum rate, that advance notice is required."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And when was that staff comment made?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. PALMORE",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0005",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "That was in -- that's been there since 1981, Justice Kennedy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Board",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Board specifically addressed this situation. It said: \"Some credit card account agreements permit the card issuer to increase the periodic rate if the consumer makes a late payment. Because the circumstances of the increase are specified in advance in the account agreement, the creditor currently need not provide a change-in-terms notice; under current 226.7(d), the new rate will appear on the periodic statement for the cycle in which the increase occurs.\""
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice GINSBURG",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the initial notice said we're going to raise it to the top, no discretion?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice KENNEDY",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0001-0013",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You talk about the plain meaning or -- I thought you agreed that the -- that the regulation is ambiguous."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government and Chase Bank",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the Government and Chase Bank argue that the word \"specific\" allows them to specify in advance only the maximum rate and that the word \"specific\" encompasses a maximum and the circumstances of causing that maximum to go in effect of a universal default situation, where if"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: So we're getting back to what was my initial question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-329",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1a781cf6b6cc935e73c067af17d9a49e115e0c51477019d2608a5f2acaa77cab",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1a781cf6b6cc-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
